    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests green. Both findings addressed \u2014 I agreed with both.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Finding 1 \u2014 duplicated SHA-256\u2192hex routine.** Agreed. Extracted a new package-private `Hashing` helper:\n- `Hashing.sha256Hex(String)` \u2192 full 64-char lower-case hex\n- `Hashing.sha256HexPrefix(String, int)` \u2192 first-N-bytes variant\n\n`OwnerIdentity.identityKey` now calls `sha256Hex`, and `HouseholdKey.householdId()` collapsed from its ~12-line digest/hex/catch block to `Hashing.sha256HexPrefix(canonical(), 6)`. The `MessageDigest`/`StandardCharsets`/`NoSuchAlgorithmException` imports are gone from `HouseholdKey`, and the `NoSuchAlgorithmException \u2192 IllegalStateException` handling now lives in exactly one place.\n\n**Finding 2 \u2014 Soundex placement.** Agreed. Moved the ~45-line American Soundex implementation (`of` + `charCode`) out of `OwnerIdentity` into its own focused `Soundex` helper, matching the `OwnerTelephones`/`HouseholdKey` idiom of one self-contained rule per class. `OwnerIdentity.identityKey` and `FlagPossibleDuplicate` now both depend on `Soundex.of(...)` directly, so the name-matching rule no longer reads as borrowed from the identity-key class. `OwnerIdentity` is back to just the key plus its two normalization helpers.\n\nI also fixed the one stale javadoc `@link` (`OwnerIdentity#soundex` \u2192 `Soundex`) created by the move. Behaviour is unchanged \u2014 the hex output and Soundex codes are identical to before, just single-sourced now, and the two acceptance tests still pass.",
