    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new pieces mostly land cleanly on the codebase's established patterns: `RejectDuplicateIdentity` mirrors `RejectCityAtCapacity` (same `service(@Val Owner, OwnerRepository)` shape, same `findAll` + skip-null-id + skip-same-id + skip-deleted loop, same checked-exception-escalates-409 convention), the exception/handler pair and its escalation YAML match the existing families exactly, `DuplicateOwnerExceptionHandler` reuses `ProblemDetails.build` like every sibling handler, and the YAML wiring slots `rejectDuplicateIdentity` in at the right point with a correct comment. Splitting `normalizedTelephone`/`lowerEmail` out as named package-private helpers on `OwnerIdentity` is a genuine readability win, and the stale `normalizedTelephone` copy in `FlagPossibleDuplicate` was correctly removed. So the additive shape is good \u2014 two structural issues keep it from LGTM.\n\n1. **Duplicated SHA-256\u2192hex routine (`OwnerIdentity.identityKey` vs `HouseholdKey.householdId`).** This diff introduces a second, near-verbatim copy of the hash-and-hex-encode block that already lives in `HouseholdKey.householdId()`:\n   - `MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\").digest(... getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8))`\n   - the `StringBuilder` + `append(String.format(\"%02x\", b))` loop\n   - the `catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException) \u2192 throw new IllegalStateException(\"SHA-256 not available\", ex)`\n\n   The only difference is full-digest vs first-6-bytes. This is exactly the kind of duplication this codebase explicitly designs against \u2014 `OwnerTelephones` and `HouseholdKey` are each documented as a \"single source of truth \u2026 so the two can never drift.\" Extract the hex-SHA-256 step into one shared helper (e.g. a small `Hashing.sha256Hex(String)` / `Hashing.sha256HexPrefix(String, int)`), and have both `identityKey` and `householdId` call it. A maintainer here would not expect two independent copies of the digest routine.\n\n2. **Soundex algorithm placement \u2014 `OwnerIdentity` is accreting a general string algorithm.** `OwnerIdentity` is documented narrowly as \"Single source of truth for an owner's identityKey,\" but now also hosts a ~45-line general-purpose American Soundex implementation (`soundex` + `charCode`), which `FlagPossibleDuplicate` reaches into purely for last-name similarity matching \u2014 a household/name-matching concern, not identity-key construction. The codebase's own idiom is to give a reusable, self-contained rule its own focused helper (`OwnerTelephones` for phone normalization, `HouseholdKey` for the household definition). Soundex fits that mold and would be more natural as its own helper (e.g. `Soundex`, or folded into the household/name-key helper), with both `OwnerIdentity` and `FlagPossibleDuplicate` depending on it directly. As written, `OwnerIdentity` is becoming a catch-all for \"all string-normalization used anywhere near identity,\" and `FlagPossibleDuplicate`'s dependency on it for name matching reads as borrowing rather than a genuine identity-key use.\n\nNeither is a correctness concern \u2014 both are about putting the logic in the right unit and not duplicating the hashing step the codebase already single-sources.",
